title: What the server serves, and what the clients add
pid: 4d8b8a84-785f-4763-984d-1b251af77623
body: |2-
  The server serves two tables over the "herald rules" endpoint
  ('docs/api-and-formats.org', "Herald rules"): the rule table, and,
  beside it, the skgrepo label table =(skgrepoLabels ("REPO" "LABEL")...)=,
  each configured skgrepo's =abbreviation=, or else its name. No
  rendered text rides in the metadata. Three heralds need what one
  headline's metadata cannot say, so each client supplies it, before
  the lens or in its place:
  - =(repoBoundary LABEL)=: the client replaces the atom =repoBoundary=
    in =(view ...)= with this form, LABEL looked up in the label table by
    the headline's =nodeRepo= (the =nodeRepo= itself when the table
    lacks it), and the rule renders it "⌂:LABEL". Where =(view ...)=
    holds =(attribution NAME [unverified])=, LABEL is "<HOME/ORIGINAL>",
    HOME that label and ORIGINAL NAME's, then "?" when unverified; the
    attribution form itself renders nothing. A headline naming no
    =nodeRepo= keeps the bare atom, which renders "⌂".
  - =pendingDetachment=: the client adds this atom to a headline that
    carries =noClaim=, is warranted by the graph (no =noWarrant=, no
    =ghostWarrant=, no =(rel removed)=), and stands under an editable
    parent: a node that is not write-protected, or a folder that is not
    write-protected whose node is such a node. The rule renders it
    "detach", styled as a request.
  - a changed folder: the server writes a folder as its bare atom, with
    no =(diff ...)=. The client sees from the folder's members whether
    some member's relationship to the folder's viewparent changed, and
    whether every such change is processed, and then draws the folder's
    herald itself instead of the lens's: ☮ when the folder is
    write-protected, its changed label ("its X changed (this file)",
    "(other files)", or "its forks changed"), and ✓ when processed, from 'folder_heralds' in
    'shared/herald-styles.json'.
  The conformance test counts =pendingDetachment= among the atoms the
  metadata may hold ('client_computed_atoms' in 'server/heralds.rs'),
  so that the rule table may render it. The code is
  'heralds-from-metadata' in 'elisp/heralds-minor-mode.el' and
  'sexp_for_lens' in 'nvim/lua/skg/heralds.lua'.
